          "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"Discernment of the times or seasons, coupled with wisdom about the appropriate course of action—often called the “Issachar anointing” in modern Christian teaching.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"1 Chronicles 12:32 — the men of Issachar are described as understanding the times and knowing what Israel should do.\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"Diligent labor, service, and burden-bearing, sometimes with the negative implication of submission to forced labor.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 49:14–15 — Jacob portrays Issachar as a strong donkey that accepts a burden and becomes a servant at forced labor.\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"Reward, recompense, or wages.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 30:18 — Leah names Issachar in connection with God giving her a reward or wages; the name is commonly explained from the Hebrew word for wages or recompense.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Agricultural prosperity, settled rest, and enjoyment of a productive land.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 49:14–15 — Issachar sees that the resting place is good and the land pleasant.\",\"Deuteronomy 33:18–19 — Issachar is blessed in its tents and associated with abundance from the seas and hidden treasures of the sand.\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"},{\"meaning\":\"Loyal participation in Israel’s leadership and warfare.\",\"basis\":[\"Judges 5:15 — Issachar’s leaders join Deborah and Barak.\",\"1 Chronicles 12:32 — Issachar supports David with informed leadership.\"],\"prominence\":\"rare\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"Issachar has no single settled symbolic meaning across historic Christian interpretation or biblical scholarship. “Understanding the times” is the most recognizable modern Christian symbolism, but it is chiefly derived from one description in 1 Chronicles 12:32. Older tribal, devotional, and typological interpretations also emphasize labor, service, reward, or prosperity.\"}"
        }
      ],
